---
import CityTime from '../../components/CityTime.astro';
import c from '../../data/cities/singapore.json';
import { history, hoursAhead, hrs, dayExtremes, hm, assertClaim, JAN, JUL, Y, sunOn, solarNoonMin, clock , hmS, hmA } from '../../lib/cityFacts';

const past = history(c.tz, 1900, Y + 1);
const last = past[past.length - 1];
assertClaim(last.year === 1982 && last.before === 450 && last.after === 480, 'Singapore moved from UTC+7:30 to UTC+8 at the start of 1982');
assertClaim(past.filter((t) => t.year > 1945 && t.year < 1982).length === 0, 'Singapore kept UTC+7:30 from 1945 to the end of 1981');
const ext = dayExtremes(c.lat, c.lon);
const dk = dayExtremes(23.76444, 90.38889);
const noons = Array.from({ length: 365 }, (_, i) => { const d = new Date(Date.UTC(Y, 0, 1 + i)); return solarNoonMin(c.tz, Y, d.getUTCMonth() + 1, d.getUTCDate(), c.lat, c.lon); });
const nMin = Math.min(...noons), nMax = Math.max(...noons);
assertClaim(nMin > 12 * 60 + 45, 'solar noon in Singapore is never before 12:45 (the text quotes the computed range)');
assertClaim(['Asia/Kuala_Lumpur', 'Asia/Hong_Kong', 'Asia/Shanghai'].every((z) => hoursAhead(c.tz, z, JAN) === 0 && hoursAhead(c.tz, z, JUL) === 0), 'Malaysia, Hong Kong and China share Singapore time');
assertClaim(ext.max - ext.min < 15, 'Singapore day length varies by less than a quarter of an hour');
const noonMar = solarNoonMin(c.tz, Y, 3, 21, c.lat, c.lon);
const june = sunOn(c.tz, c.lat, c.lon, 6, 21), dec = sunOn(c.tz, c.lat, c.lon, 12, 21);
const dhaka = hoursAhead(c.tz, 'Asia/Dhaka', JAN), lonW = hoursAhead('Europe/London', c.tz, JAN), lonS = hoursAhead('Europe/London', c.tz, JUL);
const faq = [
  { q: 'Why is Singapore on UTC+8?', a: `Singapore moved its clocks from UTC+7:30 to UTC+8 on ${last.date}, the last change in its time zone history. It has used UTC+8 without daylight saving since.` },
  { q: 'Do Kuala Lumpur, Hong Kong and Beijing share Singapore\'s clock?', a: 'Yes. Malaysia, Hong Kong and China all keep UTC+8, so their clocks match Singapore all year.' },
  { q: 'How far ahead of London is Singapore?', a: `${hrs(lonW)} hours in December or January, ${hrs(lonS)} in June or July, because the UK moves its clocks and Singapore does not.` },
  { q: 'Do sunrise and sunset change in Singapore?', a: `Hardly. Near the equator every day is roughly twelve hours long; the June solstice sunrise comes at ${june.rise}, the December one at ${dec.rise}.` },
];
---
<CityTime city={c}
  title="Singapore Time Now: SGT, UTC+8, No Daylight Saving | AtlasFigures"
  description={`Local time in Singapore right now: SGT, eight hours ahead of UTC year-round, unchanged since ${last.year}. Near-constant 12-hour days, late sunrises and hour gaps to world cities.`}
  h1="Current local time in Singapore"
  sunTitle={`Singapore's almost unchanging day, ${Y}`}
  sunSub="Sunrise, solar noon and sunset on the 21st of each month, Singapore Standard Time. Only a few minutes separate the longest and shortest days."
  diffTitle="Singapore's time gap to nine cities"
  diffSub="Hours ahead (+) or behind (−) Singapore, mid-January and mid-July. Changes come only from the other city's clocks."
  faqTitle="Singapore Standard Time explained" faq={faq}>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>Singapore runs on <strong>Singapore Standard Time, UTC+8</strong>, every day of the year. The city-state has not changed its clocks since {last.date}, when it moved forward half an hour from UTC+7:30.</p>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="story">
    <h2>When did Singapore change to UTC+8?</h2>
    <p>On {last.date}. Before that, from 1945 to the end of 1981, Singapore kept UTC+7:30. The half-hour step brought it in line with the UTC+8 used across much of East and Southeast Asia, and no later change appears in the tz data.</p>
    <h2>Why does the sun rise so late in Singapore?</h2>
    <p>Singapore lies near 103.8° east, which is closer to UTC+7 territory, but its clocks run on UTC+8. The sun therefore reaches its highest point between {clock(nMin)} and {clock(nMax)} on the clock, depending on the season: at {clock(noonMar)} on 21 March, for example. Sunrise on 21 June is at {june.rise}.</p>
    <h2>How much does day length change on the equator?</h2>
    <p>Barely. At 1.3° north, Singapore's longest day in {Y} lasts {hmS(ext.max)} and its shortest {hmS(ext.min)}: {ext.max - ext.min} minutes apart. Dhaka, by comparison, is {hrs(dhaka)} hours behind and sees a swing of about {Math.round((dk.max - dk.min) / 60)} hours.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CityTime>
